# The order the generated hooks probe for a CLI, and the order IS the contract.
#
# The checkout being pushed is the authority on its own schema, so it is probed first; an
# operator's deliberate methodology-repo export outranks the baked install path, which records
# nothing more than whichever checkout last ran `lane-start`. Probing the baked path first -- as
# every release through 0.47.1 did -- made the env branches unreachable whenever that path existed
# and was executable, so a release that ADDS an adapter-schema key could not be pushed from a
# machine whose baked CLI predated the key: the hook validated the new adapter against the old
# install's schema, `additionalProperties: false` refused it, and the only way out was --no-verify
# on a release boundary. See git_branch_liveness_hook_content's reach statement.
#
# A tuple, not an UPPER_CASE list of str: policy_phrase_constants() auto-enrolls every UPPER_CASE
# module global that is a list of str into methodology/policy-phrases.json, and these are probe
# labels, not guard-phrase vocabulary. tests/test_prepush_schema_skew.py holds the emitted
# template to exactly this sequence, for both hook names.
